{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790966799,"nonce":"pa8cl6NUJxMnIbSk9GmDMCORmxU=","title":"blinc_portal_ui's per-widget `Response.clicked` uses a global region_id→timestamp map","message":"Immediate-mode widgets need \"did THIS widget get clicked since I last rendered?\" `blinc_portal_ui` plumbs this via:\n\n1. `install_click_hook(kit)` registers an additive `add_click_listener` on the kit. On every click the listener stamps a process-global `HashMap\u003cregion_id, Instant\u003e` with `Instant::now()`.\n2. Each widget allocates its rect + computes a stable `WidgetId` (hash of `id_stack + per-frame call_counter`) → `region_id` string.\n3. `allocate_painter` reads + REMOVES the entry: `clicks().lock().unwrap().remove(\u0026region_id).is_some()` — `Response.clicked = true` exactly once for the first frame that observes the stamp.\n\n**Why consume-on-read instead of \"stamp \u003e= last_frame_started\":** the timestamp approach failed in practice. Even when the kit fired `on_click_cb` cleanly, the compare against a per-portal `last_frame_started: Instant` was racy across frame boundaries — the click would either be missed (stamp older than the captured frame anchor) or fire repeatedly (stamp newer than every subsequent anchor) depending on event-loop scheduling. Consume-on-read sidesteps it: the first widget paint that sees the entry takes it, no clock arithmetic.\n\n**How to apply:** Any new portal widget that needs click detection just calls `self.allocate_painter(size, Sense::Click)` and reads `resp.clicked`. Don't reintroduce timestamp compares. Stamps for widgets that disappeared between frames stay in the map until something with the same id re-renders and consumes them — imgui's \"no ask, no get\" semantics, and self-pruning enough that the map doesn't grow unbounded in practice.\n\nDepends on [[gotcha-canvas-kit-on-element-click-single-owner]] — the additive `add_click_listener` is the only way to keep the stamping hook alive when the host crate also subscribes to click events.","files":null}]}